WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:03.010
- Nature, the most powerful

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.423
creative force on Earth.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.490
I'm Chef Melissa King.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.800
Cooking has taken me to incredible places.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.020
Wow. (laughs)

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.050
From TV competitions and celebrity galas

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.390
to countries around the world.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.300
I'm heading out to places
I've never been before

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.190
to seek out new experiences

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.950
and to create new dishes
inspired by nature.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.410
Wow. This is amazing.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.050
I'm teaming up with some of
the world's greatest explorers

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.850
to go further than I've ever gone before.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.190
I love how excited you get
about fungal organisms.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.023
- Oh yeah.

00:00:37.023 --> 00:00:40.780
- To push my craft,
creativity, and my cooking.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.393
This is Tasting Wild.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.060
Rainforests, they cover
just 6% of our planet,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.628
but are so important.

00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:03.970
(birds chirping)

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.460
Not only do they bring
balance to our environment

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.400
by filtering the air we
breathe, but they're home

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.650
to some of the world's most unique

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.603
and important biodiversity.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.523
Including some species that
are staples in my kitchen.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.963
We're here in the Olympic National Forest,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.920
home to one of the only
temperate rain forests

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.933
in the continental United States,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.440
and I'm here to meet Korena Mafune.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.660
She's a fungal ecologist,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.223
and she studies one of my
favorite ingredients, mushrooms.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.040
I'm meeting Korena in what's
known as an old growth forest,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.050
this one actually being a rainforest,

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.150
and we're goin' on a hike.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.590
Hey Korena.
- Hey Melissa.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.423
It's so good to meet you.

00:01:57.423 --> 00:01:58.750
- Hi, you too.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.433
- [Korena] Welcome to
Olympic National Forest.

00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:02.170
- This is gorgeous.
- Yeah.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.081
Are you up for a hike?
- I would love that.

00:02:04.081 --> 00:02:06.250
(birds chirping)

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.900
- I'm Korena Mafune,

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.789
I'm a fungal ecologist, and
I study the interactions

00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:15.760
between plants and fungi
and the soil systems.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.210
I love being out here in these
beautiful old growth forests.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.460
It just gives me this sense of balance

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.670
and I'm so lucky to be
able to call it my office.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.160
Fungal organisms are so important.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.693
They are everywhere we look.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.160
The ones that we do see
are usually mushrooms,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.200
and mushrooms come in all shapes and forms

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.920
and they fulfill so many
different ecological roles.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.310
They have been here for so
long, much longer than us

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.360
and they provide a
balance in the ecosystem.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.450
I'm so excited to learn about Melissa

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.210
and her utilization of
mushrooms in the kitchen

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.860
and learn about that perspective.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.840
So our plan is to start at Merriman Falls

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.730
and work our way down Merriman Creek,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.260
which feeds into Lake Quinault,

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.440
and we're hopefully gonna
find some really cool things.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.510
But you have to go through some difficulty

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.090
to get to these places
that are just so beautiful

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.590
and will hold all the little secrets

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.409
that you see around every corner.

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:16.630
(majestic music)

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.303
- Wow. It's so magical.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.563
And the moss is insane.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.760
So what defines an old growth forest?

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.220
- So the moss that you see
draping off these trees

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.500
is one of the defining characteristics

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.770
and then it really has to do
with the age of the trees,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.950
so 150, 200 plus years old.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.141
- [Melissa] So they're old.

00:03:40.141 --> 00:03:41.080
- They're old. Yeah.
- They're like ancient.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.750
- Some trees out here are
well over 500 years old,

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.820
and the ones that I climb are usually

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.593
up towards 200 years old.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.660
- So you spend time up in these trees?

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.640
- I climb up to the tippy top.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.760
And there's soils on tree branches.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.580
A lot of people come to
see these mats of moss

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.290
that are hanging off the trees.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.150
- Little magical fairy land.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.040
- But the real treasure is actually

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.520
what is under those mossy
mats that we call canopy soil.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:10.480
- What am I holding here?

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.000
- Yeah, so canopy soil, it is a soil

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.310
and it is so rich in nutrients.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:19.720
So under those beautiful
draping mats of moss

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.420
we actually have this organic
matter that accumulates

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.280
and the trees are growing roots

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.890
out of their branches
into those mats of moss.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.230
It's an uptake nutrients from those mats.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.130
- Incredible.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.930
There's so much life here that's happening

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.430
beyond what we can see.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.588
You really have the coolest
job in the whole world.

00:04:35.588 --> 00:04:38.890
- You have a pretty cool job. (laughs)

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.680
- So where do we start
to hunt for mushrooms?

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.720
- The waterfall would be a
really great place to start

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.520
because we can follow it down the river

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.930
and I think there'll be some mushrooms

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.196
that we can find down there.

00:04:48.196 --> 00:04:51.029
(uplifting music)

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.520
- Mushrooms tend to anchor a dish.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.560
They carry so much earthiness.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.030
They're very versatile as well.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.430
You can cook 'em fresh, use them dried.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.220
You can turn 'em into powders
and infuse 'em into broths,

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.073
but they add a boost of
flavor to your dishes.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.880
- [Korena] What is really
beautiful about the balance

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.940
of these ecosystems, it is
the circle of life out here.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.700
So the mushrooms are decaying this,

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.460
providing habitat to so many organisms.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:19.390
- [Melissa] It's beautiful.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.070
It's just this sense of rebirth

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:22.900
happening right here in front of us.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:23.913
- [Korena] Yeah.

00:05:23.913 --> 00:05:27.400
(uplifting music)

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.650
- [Melissa] I'm that
person that gets distracted

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:30.700
when I'm on a hike-

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.930
- Always.
- I'm always like staring

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.150
at things on the ground.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:39.150
I see some, sorrel here
and some licorice fern.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.783
I've made tea with this.

00:05:42.507 --> 00:05:44.380
It is a great licoricy flavor.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.320
- Literally, it's called
licorice fern. (laughs)

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.000
Oh, look what we have here.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.735
- Whoa, what?

00:05:50.735 --> 00:05:52.000
- Look at this.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.616
Yeah, so this is a type of bolete,

00:05:56.616 --> 00:05:58.930
so the main one that you
might be familiar with

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.740
is boletus edulis, which
is the porcini mushroom.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.060
- [Melissa] Looks a little bit different.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.710
- Yeah, we won't wanna eat this one,

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:08.290
but if we sample this guy

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:10.063
you can see the pores there.

00:06:10.063 --> 00:06:11.080
- Like spongy.
- Kind of sponge.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.580
Yeah, it's spongy.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.050
There are just billions

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.566
of little spores in those little tubes.

00:06:17.566 --> 00:06:20.710
- [Melissa] Oh, this is
cool. This is gorgeous.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.053
- All right, let's keep going.
- All right.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:29.200
I'm just excited to keep
going deeper into the forest.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.780
It's so serene driving through the woods.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.896
Everywhere you look, there's
something beautiful to see.

00:06:34.896 --> 00:06:38.170
(dramatic music)

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.210
You can see the full circle here,

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.820
life, but you can also see decay.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.320
Everything here seems to be working

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.183
in harmony everywhere I look.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.600
Yeah, this forest that we're
driving through is so beautiful

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.690
and I love the sense of balance you get

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.540
as a person when you're
standing in it all.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.670
You could feel the trees like breathing.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.530
You could feel the whole ecosystem-

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:04.810
- Yeah.
- Just coming alive.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.903
- [Korena] I'm excited
to see what you cook.

00:07:07.856 --> 00:07:08.710
- [Melissa] It's a good partnership.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.660
You find the mushrooms, I'll cook 'em.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.960
- Let's get out there and ride
- Let's do it.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:18.343
You lead the way.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.240
- So this is a great place
to take a look around

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.473
because we're not too
far off from the stream.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.193
Let's get over on the other side.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:45.040
Yes.
- Oh my god, they're so cute.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.720
- These are oyster mushrooms.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.916
Do you make any additional odd
dishes with oyster mushrooms?

00:07:48.916 --> 00:07:51.310
- I cook a lot with
every kind of mushroom.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.290
- Every kind of mushroom?
- I don't discriminate.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:54.580
- Neither do.
- I love 'em all.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.990
- I'm an equal mushroom opportunist,

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:57.130
so that's great.
- I love that.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:58.270
- When you see mushrooms,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.280
they're just kind of like
the fruit of the fungus,

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:01.840
like an apple to an apple tree.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.070
So the actual fungal organism
is all throughout this log,

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.020
breaking it down and decomposing it

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.690
and recycling those
nutrients into the forest.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:11.533
- Amazing.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.340
I love mushrooms, but I
know that there's moments

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.170
where they're not safe to eat.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:20.320
- I always tell people, "If
in doubt, throw it out."

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.577
And we kind of always nerd out and say,

00:08:22.577 --> 00:08:25.120
"You can eat all mushrooms,
but some you'll only eat once,"

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.220
because there are

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.660
poisonous mushrooms.
(Melissa hums)

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:30.520
These ones are definitely safe to eat.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:31.690
They are a little young,

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:34.960
but there's some clusters up
here that look pretty good.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:35.793
- How do we go in?

00:08:35.793 --> 00:08:38.080
- I would kind of just saw it right there.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.030
- Right underneath?

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:42.850
I like feel bad taking too many.

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:45.400
- [Korena] I always leave a few behind

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:47.470
because leaving some behind

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.230
allows those spores to continue to drop,

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:52.870
and so you're not taking all
the reproductive structures

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.380
and you're allowing them to
return that to the ecosystem.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.870
- Just capturing them in that peak moment,

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:00.880
to me as a chef, is so special,

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.610
and really maintain the
integrity of that ingredient.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:06.373
I think is what I enjoy doing most.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.120
I'd love to see what else we can find.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.610
- [Korena] All right. Let's
keep going down along the river.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.530
- Yeah, let's go check it out.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.690
As a chef, we see mushrooms
come in the kitchen

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:19.210
all the time, but to see them growing here

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.400
in its natural habitat,
there's something special

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.580
about being able to harvest
your food right on a log.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.550
This whole terrain is making me very happy

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:28.840
and being able to see this.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.570
- Yeah, this is another oyster mushroom

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.240
likely the same species
that we saw earlier,

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.700
but this has had time to mature.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.730
As you can see, if we lift it up,

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:44.020
we see those gills that are
holding those fungal spores.

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.087
- Flavor gets in there when you cook 'em.

00:09:46.087 --> 00:09:47.433
(Melissa giggles)
- Yeah.

00:09:47.433 --> 00:09:50.980
- Aw, smells so good.
It's making me hungry.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:52.030
Can we harvest this one?

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:53.320
- Yeah. It's ready to go.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.260
(uplifting music)

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.080
- It's beautiful. It's magic.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.490
Nature's always the place where I find

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:02.920
the most inspiration in my cooking.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.330
Finding mushrooms like this

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:07.660
and being able to really tell
their story through my dish.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:10.570
Thinking of maybe making
some sort of mushroom broth

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:14.710
or a kanji with this to kind
of bring in some of my roots.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.470
I mean, you did mention
roots and the root system,

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:21.583
so I'm thinking bringing it
back to to home and my heritage.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.520
I can't wait to cook for you.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.110
Let's go.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.320
Cooking mushrooms for someone
that studies mushrooms,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.120
I wanna get them right.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.065
All of the things that
we've gathered together

00:10:33.065 --> 00:10:34.870
in the past couple days

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.760
I wanna incorporate those ingredients

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:39.790
and build the layers to this dish.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.867
Kanji is the chicken noodle
soup of Asian culture.

00:10:42.867 --> 00:10:45.820
It reminds me of my
family, and I want Korena

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:49.403
to understand who I am
as she tastes my food.

00:10:49.403 --> 00:10:52.236
(uplifting music)

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.450
Yesterday, was incredible,
and just thank you so much

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.480
for teaching me about all the fungi.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.765
The way you talk about forests
is the way I talk about food.

00:11:07.765 --> 00:11:08.598
(Korena and Melissa laughing)

00:11:08.598 --> 00:11:09.820
- [Korena] Yeah, I'm so curious

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:11.530
to see what you come up with.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:14.050
- I'm so excited to take you
to this really great spot

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.480
I found right in the
forest and create a dish

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.660
that really shows what
this place is all about.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.103
- [Korena] I'm so excited. (laughs)

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.257
- I love the forest so much.
Help me grab some things.

00:11:29.257 --> 00:11:32.174
(mysterious music)

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:38.533
Here it is.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.160
- [Korena] Oh, this is beautiful.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:43.900
- [Korena] I thought you'd like this spot

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.270
and you can go ahead and
take those mushrooms out.

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:49.133
- Oh, so many beautiful mushrooms.

00:11:49.133 --> 00:11:51.190
- I have a lot of my
favorite kinds as well

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.050
as the ones that we foraged yesterday.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:55.390
- Yeah, I see some of my favorites,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.093
the morel, chanterelles,
we got the shiitakes.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:11:59.890
- [Melissa] All right.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.710
I thought it'd be great
to cook a rice porridge

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.090
mushroom kanji for you.

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.883
- [Korena] Yeah, that sounds delicious.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:11.263
- [Melissa] Start off with
some aromatics, onions, garlic.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:14.830
You keep an eye on that.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.450
I'll grate some ginger,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.630
which is really the base of this dish.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:21.430
Toss the rice in.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:22.960
- Stir that in?
- Yeah, toast that up.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.910
It's like making a risotto.
- Yeah.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:27.400
- But we're doing a
Chinese version of that.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:32.080
We made this mushroom
broth with all the stems,

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.860
some kombu, and just slow cook that down.

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:38.500
This is the base of our kanji.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.420
I'm gonna cut some mushrooms,

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:43.155
each with their own personality.

00:12:43.155 --> 00:12:46.390
Oh, these are some of the
oysters we had from yesterday.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.070
- Oh, yeah.
- We have to cook these up.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:50.650
- Gotta cut 'em up.
- It's so cool.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:53.410
So I'm gonna saute these mushrooms next,

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:56.980
throwing them all together
to build a harmonious dish.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.440
Each mushroom has its own unique flavor

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.880
and I love caramelizing them
getting a beautiful sear on it.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.510
It really brings out the flavor
and kind of deepens them.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.510
When it comes to making
kanji, at the end of the day,

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.300
it's about finding the
balance between the softness

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:16.870
of the rice and then the
textures from the garnishes.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:19.753
Again, it's about that harmony.
It's about that balance.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.320
Because we're close to the coast here,

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:27.550
I've decided to put some
dull seaweed as a garnish.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.950
All of these beautiful
caramelized mushrooms

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.480
are gonna go right on top.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.400
These are some beautiful little herbs

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.232
we foraged together from the sorrel.

00:13:36.232 --> 00:13:40.120
I wanted it to just feel like the forest.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:42.610
- Like a forest in a bowl.
- Yeah.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.620
Final touches some fried shallots

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:45.850
for some textures.
- Yes.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.220
- Add a little crunch and crispiness.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:51.313
Some green onions to add a little kick.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:57.790
Here's your mushroom kanji.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:00.259
Hope it does your love
of mushroom's justice.

00:14:00.259 --> 00:14:03.033
- It's beautiful.

00:14:03.033 --> 00:14:04.650
- All right.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:05.620
- Do you stir it up?

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:06.453
- Sure.
- Yeah.

00:14:06.453 --> 00:14:08.083
- [Melissa] Okay. Get them in there.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:13.843
- That's so good. That's so good.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:16.390
I do eat mushrooms and
cook with them a lot,

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:19.240
but I haven't eaten them in this manner

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.033
and in from such a talented
chef, so thank you.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.810
The flavors, they just,
they evolve in my mouth

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.910
kind of like the forest that has evolved

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.590
around us over millions of years.

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.350
It's like every little
bite has a little secret,

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.053
just kind of every corner
in the forest that you turn.

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:38.590
- I'm gonna have to
toast you to mushrooms.

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:40.240
- I'm gonna have to toast
you to cooking, chef.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:41.890
Thank you.
- (laughs) Thank you.

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:45.970
This forest is magical.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.940
It's such a beautiful
place to explore and cook.

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.430
You can't help but be energized here.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:54.337
I'm taking away a deeper
appreciation for mushrooms

00:14:54.337 --> 00:14:57.070
and the balance they provide,
and I'm looking forward

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.145
to taking that all back to the kitchen.

00:14:59.145 --> 00:15:01.978
(inspiring music)

